# Code owners for local-operator.
#
# 2026-09-16 -- NO CODE OWNERS, AND NO AUTOMATIC REVIEW REQUESTS (operator
# decision). This file used to end with a `*` line naming five owners. GitHub
# creates a review request for every handle on a pattern that matches a pull
# request's changed files, at the moment the PR opens, and that request pinged
# `bbqben`, `olafagbemi`, `sherman-tsui` and `SanaKetabchi` on every PR this
# repository opened -- usually while the diff was still churning through
# remediation rounds, so four people were notified per PR about work that was
# not yet worth reading. There is no GitHub setting that keeps an ownership map
# and drops the request: matching a pattern IS the request. The pattern is
# therefore gone, and this path is kept, pattern-free, as the record of the
# change and the reason for it.
#
# What had to move with it, because a code-owner list and a code-owner rule are
# two halves of one mechanism:
#
#   * The `Main Protection` ruleset (id 3622629) no longer sets
#     `require_code_owner_review`. It still requires
#     `required_approving_review_count: 1`, so an outside contributor's PR still
#     cannot reach `main` without a collaborator's approval -- the tier-2
#     protection described in AGENTS.md, "Who may merge: two tiers". Leaving the
#     code-owner rule on with nobody declared would have made the approval
#     requirement unsatisfiable and pushed every merge through the configured
#     admin bypass -- the exact failure this file was originally added to fix
#     (PRs #686 and #688-#696, 2026-09-05/06, all merged at
#     `reviewDecision: REVIEW_REQUIRED` with zero approvals).
#   * Pull requests are opened **non-draft** again. The draft step existed only
#     to suppress the code-owner request while a PR was still in flux; with no
#     patterns to match there is nothing to suppress, and a draft only delays the
#     merge, since a draft PR cannot be merged at all.
#
# The review itself is unchanged in substance: an independent reviewer
# subagent's round, posted on the PR, repeated until no blocker or major remains
# and fresh against the head, is still what authorizes a merge here -- with
# `--admin` disclosed on the PR when the ruleset's self-approval limitation
# forces it (see AGENTS.md, "Who may merge: two tiers").
#
# When a human review IS wanted it is now a deliberate act rather than a side
# effect of opening a PR: add the reviewer by hand at the point the PR is
# actually ready to be read, `gh pr edit <n> --add-reviewer <handle>`. Who to ask
# -- the handles whose judgement stands in for the project's: `damianvtran`
# (admin), `bbqben`, `olafagbemi`, `sherman-tsui`, `SanaKetabchi`. One mechanical
# difference since 2026-09-16: the ruleset's `required_approving_review_count: 1`
# is satisfied by an approving review from *any* collaborator with write access,
# not only from those five, because no code owners are declared any more. Ask the
# five by preference. `sebkow` and `jcobhams` also hold write access but are
# deliberately NOT in that set: ownership here means "can approve on behalf of
# the project", which is narrower than commit access. Verify a handle before
# relying on it with `gh api repos/damianvtran/local-operator/collaborators`,
# since a handle without write access is silently ignored by GitHub.
#
# Validate any future edit with GitHub's own parser rather than by eye, and pin
# the ref or it silently validates the default branch instead of your branch:
# `gh api 'repos/damianvtran/local-operator/codeowners/errors?ref=<branch>'` (an
# empty error list means the file parsed).
